sequenceDiagram
  %% Target design: B6 slot design (migration-dashboard/drafts/task-vm-slots.md, M1), docs/reference/compute-drivers.md (Task VM slots), docs/reference/task-vm-native-provision.md, DECISIONS R1-R4.
  %% Tightened: flow-tighten rules 1, 2, 3, 6, 9, 14, 15, 16, 20, 22, 27 (enrolment, hub peer, keys and model key are gone from the claim; the VM steps are the boot workflow that the enrolment's proof VM uses; one ready check that waits for the identity unit; the nested topology is one call to a shared workflow).
  %% Changed in M4: the claim is a child workflow of the task's own `tasks.provision` run, as `task-vm-provision` is; the boot is one call to `task-vm-slot-boot` (rule 27); the claim removes the slot Node's old facts; the fetch takes the start commit.
  %% status: reviewed
  %% command: none · task VM claim for a task
  %% kind: task_vm.slot_claim
  autonumber
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane H icon=server pill=target tone=amber
  %% lane VM icon=server pill=target tone=purple
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>workflow execution
  participant H as Task VM host<br/>over ssh
  participant VM as Slot VM<br/>task image
  Note over GW: Validate, take a free slot
  %%> Started by:
  %%> - the `tasks:provision` flow, as its first step
  %%> - there is no CLI command of its own
  %%> Requirements:
  %%> - an active task VM host has a `free` slot
  %%> - the task has no other slot
  %%> - the host is listed in `task_vms.slot_claim_hosts` (none is by default); any other host enrols a new VM for the task, as before
  %%> - one SQLite transaction takes the slot, so two claims never get the same one
  %%> Chooses:
  %%> - the host with the most free slots, then its lowest-numbered free slot
  %%> - the current task image, or the one-day fallback image
  %%> - a host that claims slots never creates a VM of its own; a host that does not claim slots is used only when no claiming host has a free slot
  %%> Result:
  %%> - the slot becomes `provisioning` and records the task, `claimed_at` and the image
  %%> - no Node, address, token or key is made: the slot already has them
  %%> - the slot Node's old agent facts are removed in the same transaction (an unchanged report is never rewritten, so an old fact would stay)
  %%> - a task is recorded with the full intent
  %%> When it waits:
  %%> - no host has a free slot: the task waits (`capacity`) and starts when a slot is released
  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-task-provision
  %%> Why this run:
  %%> - the claim is the child workflow `task-vm-slot-claim`; the run's workspace steps follow it, as they follow `task-vm-provision`
  %%> Inputs:
  %%> - the slot name, host, project and pool
  %%> - the image fingerprint, the seed volume names and the resource limits
  %%> - the static bridge address and the fixed MAC
  %%> - the pinned observer and Pi digests
  %%> - the task branch and its commit
  %%> Secrets:
  %%> - none travel in this run; the identity volume on the host holds them
  SW->>H: Boot the VM<br/>workflow · task-vm-slot-boot
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - the boot that an enrolment's proof VM uses, as ssh exec argvs on the host
  %%> - removes a stale VM and seed clone of the slot (none exists for a free slot)
  %%> - copies the image's topology seed to `HOST-tvm-N-topology-seed`; the seed holds the three stopped nested VMs (gateway, app-dev, app-prod)
  %%> - creates the VM with the instance config on stdin: the task image, the seed clone, the identity volume as a read-only disk at `/opt/orbit-slot`, the static address, the fixed MAC, the limits and the user data for the account
  %%> - starts it, then waits until the guest has its address
  SW->>VM: Check the slot VM<br/>ssh exec · pinned host key
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - runs `systemctl start orbit-slot.target && systemctl is-active --quiet orbit-slot.target`, within the boot allowance, then one command
  %%> - the observer and Pi binaries match their pinned digests
  %%> - the observer answers, and Pi answers its capabilities probe
  %%> Why:
  %%> - the identity unit installs the identity before ssh, WireGuard and the observer start, then reloads systemd and starts the target again; the target starts WireGuard, the observer and the Pi unit of the tree, with no per-VM write
  %%> - the target is bound to WireGuard and Pi, so a failed or skipped start of either fails the target, and a later stop of either takes it down
  %%> - the Incus agent answers about 4 s earlier, so it is not the signal
  %%> - there is no repair: the task image is refreshed after every release, so a digest that differs fails the step
  VM-->>GW: First host report<br/>orbit-agent · host fact
  %%> Why:
  %%> - the Gateway keeps the latest fact per Node, kind and subject
  %%> - the claim removed the old facts, so a `host` fact received after `claimed_at` proves the new VM reports
  SW->>VM: Fetch the task branch<br/>ssh exec · git fetch
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - fetches the task's start commit onto the checkout at the workspace path, when the task image holds one
  %%> - only the new commits travel
  %%> - skipped for a public repository, which needs no credential; the workspace steps clone it as they do without a slot
  %%> Credential:
  %%> - the Gateway mints a short-lived GitHub token when it admits the task
  %%> - it passes the token through the vault as a key name, never as a value
  %%> - the fetch gets the token on stdin and in one process's environment, never in argv, and does not keep it on disk
  SW->>VM: Reset the nested topology<br/>workflow · task-vm-nested-reset
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - runs on the slot VM's own nested Incus, as `ssh exec` argvs
  %%> - copies the three ready VMs (gateway, app-dev, app-prod) from the stopped originals in the seed clone (btrfs copy, seconds)
  %%> - starts them
  %%> - applies the candidate code: migrations, then a Swamp reload
  %%> - one readiness check: fresh agent facts from the nested Nodes, and the nested Gateway answers on `10.45.0.1`
  %%> When it runs:
  %%> - only while the Gateway setting `nested_reset` is on; it is off, and the step is skipped, until the workflow lands with B3/B4 (a placeholder workflow fails if the setting is turned on early)
  %%> Why one call:
  %%> - one shared workflow keeps the claim short
  %%> - it touches only the three nested VMs
  alt every step passes
    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event
    %%> How the Gateway learns it:
    %%> - its run reader sees the terminal event; Swamp never calls the Gateway
    Note over GW: Record result
    %%> Result:
    %%> - the slot becomes `ready` once a `host` fact received after `claimed_at` has arrived; the workspace steps run first, so the first report is normally in
    %%> - when none has arrived, the task fails with `task_vm.slot_not_reporting` and the slot becomes `failed`
    %%> - the task succeeds, with the nested topology up on the candidate code
    %%> Next:
    %%> - the `tasks:provision` flow sets up the workspace on the slot's Node
  else a step fails
    H--xSW: exit ≠ 0 or timeout
    %%> When:
    %%> - a host command, the ready check, the boot allowance, the fetch or the nested reset fails
    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step
    Note over GW: Mark the slot failed
    %%> Result:
    %%> - the slot is `failed`, so no claim can take it, and an alert fires
    %%> - the task fails and the group asks for assistance
    %%> On the host:
    %%> - the VM (with its nested VMs) and the seed clone stay until the release runs; there is no rollback
    %%> Retry:
    %%> - cancel the group; `task_vm.slot_release` deletes the VM and frees the slot, and a new claim starts a new task
  end
